1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:02,050
Three different games.

2
00:00:02,070 --> 00:00:03,250
The same crash,

3
00:00:03,270 --> 00:00:04,130
on the same line

4
00:00:04,150 --> 00:00:05,650
of a sound library.

5
00:00:05,670 --> 00:00:07,410
No single store console would

6
00:00:07,430 --> 00:00:08,480
ever show you that.

7
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:11,100
We are Rui Codex,

8
00:00:11,120 --> 00:00:12,780
a small studio in Belgium,

9
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:14,620
with 34 apps on Google

10
00:00:14,640 --> 00:00:16,220
Play and the App Store.

11
00:00:16,240 --> 00:00:18,300
Our feedback is not a flood.

12
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:19,660
It is a trickle:

13
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:22,380
51 written reviews in 9 languages,

14
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:25,420
42 crash issues and 68 releases,

15
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:27,260
scattered across two stores and

16
00:00:27,280 --> 00:00:29,180
a crash console nobody opens

17
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:30,010
on a Monday.

18
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:32,380
So during this hackathon

19
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:34,380
we built Storefront Steward:

20
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:35,420
an agent that runs

21
00:00:35,440 --> 00:00:37,180
the studio's morning pass,

22
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:38,540
with one rule.

23
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:39,980
Nothing reaches a store

24
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:41,370
without a human click.

25
00:00:42,040 --> 00:00:43,860
One click starts the pass.

26
00:00:43,880 --> 00:00:45,220
Muse Spark plans it,

27
00:00:45,240 --> 00:00:46,740
and calls our own tools:

28
00:00:46,760 --> 00:00:48,420
a read-only store sync,

29
00:00:48,440 --> 00:00:52,020
triage, grouping, reply drafts and tickets.

30
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:53,620
Every step, and its cost,

31
00:00:53,640 --> 00:00:55,090
appears as it happens.

32
00:00:55,730 --> 00:00:56,670
Every review,

33
00:00:56,690 --> 00:00:57,790
crash and release of the

34
00:00:57,810 --> 00:00:59,070
whole studio fits in one

35
00:00:59,090 --> 00:01:01,070
Muse Spark context.

36
00:01:01,090 --> 00:01:02,350
No retrieval.

37
00:01:02,370 --> 00:01:05,150
It returns issues as strict JSON,

38
00:01:05,170 --> 00:01:06,670
and plain code checks

39
00:01:06,690 --> 00:01:08,110
every cited id,

40
00:01:08,130 --> 00:01:09,230
and every quote,

41
00:01:09,250 --> 00:01:10,190
word for word,

42
00:01:10,210 --> 00:01:11,950
before anything is shown.

43
00:01:11,970 --> 00:01:14,190
It also remembers the last pass,

44
00:01:14,210 --> 00:01:14,830
so it can tell

45
00:01:14,850 --> 00:01:15,900
you what is new.

46
00:01:16,530 --> 00:01:18,030
Then it drafts replies,

47
00:01:18,050 --> 00:01:20,190
in the reviewer's own language.

48
00:01:20,210 --> 00:01:20,830
Here,

49
00:01:20,850 --> 00:01:22,670
Spark promised that ads would

50
00:01:22,690 --> 00:01:25,070
become skippable within five seconds.

51
00:01:25,090 --> 00:01:26,350
Only a release note

52
00:01:26,370 --> 00:01:27,870
may promise a feature.

53
00:01:27,890 --> 00:01:29,950
The verifier blocked the draft,

54
00:01:29,970 --> 00:01:31,550
sent the reason back once,

55
00:01:31,570 --> 00:01:33,100
and the revision passed.

56
00:01:33,810 --> 00:01:35,950
On 120 held-out reviews

57
00:01:35,970 --> 00:01:37,310
in 10 languages,

58
00:01:37,330 --> 00:01:40,590
117 of 119 drafts

59
00:01:40,610 --> 00:01:42,190
passed first time.

60
00:01:42,210 --> 00:01:43,710
Building that test found a

61
00:01:43,730 --> 00:01:45,310
defect in our own prompt,

62
00:01:45,330 --> 00:01:46,590
and a false alarm

63
00:01:46,610 --> 00:01:47,820
in our own checker.

64
00:01:48,390 --> 00:01:49,730
When a reply is approved,

65
00:01:49,750 --> 00:01:51,250
one click posts it.

66
00:01:51,270 --> 00:01:52,290
This one is now

67
00:01:52,310 --> 00:01:53,810
live on Google Play,

68
00:01:53,830 --> 00:01:54,800
word for word.

69
00:01:55,570 --> 00:01:58,030
For engineering, it writes the ticket.

70
00:01:58,050 --> 00:01:59,470
One fix in the sound

71
00:01:59,490 --> 00:02:01,580
library reaches three games.

72
00:02:01,650 --> 00:02:02,350
Approve it,

73
00:02:02,370 --> 00:02:03,310
and it is exported

74
00:02:03,330 --> 00:02:04,300
for the tracker.

75
00:02:05,010 --> 00:02:06,030
For the store listing,

76
00:02:06,050 --> 00:02:08,590
Muse Image paints only the scene.

77
00:02:08,610 --> 00:02:10,750
SAM finds the phone's screen,

78
00:02:10,770 --> 00:02:12,030
and our code places

79
00:02:12,050 --> 00:02:13,950
the untouched app capture.

80
00:02:13,970 --> 00:02:16,910
A guard compares every interface pixel.

81
00:02:16,930 --> 00:02:18,750
The difference must be zero,

82
00:02:18,770 --> 00:02:20,620
or the image is thrown away.

83
00:02:21,640 --> 00:02:23,220
Personal data is removed

84
00:02:23,240 --> 00:02:24,820
before any model call.

85
00:02:24,840 --> 00:02:26,580
And triage itself runs

86
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:27,860
on open weights:

87
00:02:27,880 --> 00:02:30,900
we distilled Spark into Muse Glimmer.

88
00:02:30,920 --> 00:02:35,700
0.993 macro-F1, against the teacher's 0.991,

89
00:02:35,720 --> 00:02:37,460
at a fifth of the cost.

90
00:02:37,480 --> 00:02:38,660
It even runs on

91
00:02:38,680 --> 00:02:39,940
a single PC,

92
00:02:39,960 --> 00:02:41,170
with no network.

93
00:02:41,850 --> 00:02:43,110
Every call is priced

94
00:02:43,130 --> 00:02:44,230
before it is sent,

95
00:02:44,250 --> 00:02:46,230
and written to a receipt.

96
00:02:46,250 --> 00:02:47,670
The whole build used under

97
00:02:47,690 --> 00:02:50,420
$10 of Meta API credit.

98
00:02:51,660 --> 00:02:53,000
Storefront Steward.

99
00:02:53,020 --> 00:02:54,440
The studio's feedback,

100
00:02:54,460 --> 00:02:55,560
read in one place,

101
00:02:55,580 --> 00:02:57,350
and answered with a human click.
